  total blue magnitude, HI flux, HI systemic velocity and the 20% line width, for 44 objects.
  PRC classes: A = kinematically CONFIRMED polar ring; B = good candidate; C = possible candidate;
               D = possibly related object (mergers, peculiars -- not polar rings).
  THE LOAD-BEARING ASSUMPTION, stated up front: in a polar-ring galaxy the HI lies overwhelmingly in the RING,
  not in the gas-poor early-type host, so W20 measures the RING's rotation.  That is the standard reading of
  these observations and it is why the survey was done; it is also what makes this a test of item 49 at all.
  The ring is assumed edge-on (sin i = 1), which is how PRC objects are recognised.  Any i < 90 deg would raise
  the inferred rotation speed, so every velocity below is a LOWER bound.
  
